// `::` resolves NAMESPACES and TYPES; `.` is instance/value access (SPEC: "a `::` head is always a
// type/namespace, a `.` head always a value"). The emitter used to quietly accept a value-headed `::`
// and lower it to the same C as `.`, which gave the language a second, undocumented way to spell field
// access — exactly what GOALS.md's "favor one way to do a thing" rules out. It is now an error.
// The legitimate `::` forms all keep a TYPE/NAMESPACE head and are unaffected: `Vec2::dot(...)` (static
// fn), `Type::NAME` (comptime const), `Color::Blue` (enum variant), `ns::counter` (module static).
type value Inner { public int32 deep; }
type value Outer { public Inner inner; }

fn int32 main() {
    Outer o;
    return o::inner::deep;   // ERROR: `o` is a value — write `o.inner.deep`
}
